{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man shall have in his skin, etc.</b> That is, on the path of what they said, of blessed memory (Bava Metzia 114a): ״You are called adam, and the nations of the world are not called adam״ — for the nations do not become impure through nega'im. From here there is a proof that gentiles do not become impure through nega'im, as they said in tractate Nega'im at the beginning of chapter twelve." "And its saying <b>when it shall be in the skin of his flesh</b> — the verse informs us that the impurity of tzara'at upon the Israelite man comes only in the skin of his flesh and not internally, for the nations' dimension of soul itself is the dimension of tzara'at and of their kind, and in the soul of Israel, when it encounters them on the side of the sin that he may sin, the appearance of tzara'at will inherit [manifest] in the skin of the flesh — and specifically in his skin and not in his flesh, and needless to say not in his interior." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Arakhin 3a) that the reason it says ״adam״ is to include even a minor, and also to include a woman, for she too is called adam, as it is written (Genesis 5:2): ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶת שְׁמָם אָדָם״." "Further, they said in Torat Kohanim that the reason it says <b>in the skin of his flesh</b> is to include even skin that has no hair follicles — meaning, lest you say it applies only in a place fit to grow hair, but in a place that has no hair follicles, even though hair grew there and turned white, it does not render impure — therefore it is stated. In any case, the baheret must precede the white hair. But if white hair preceded the baheret — for example, an old man whose hair turned white, or a garmini (one whose nature is thus), he is pure. And if the baheret preceded the hair, and when the hair grew it grew white within the baheret, there is room to doubt whether it is required that the growth of the hair precede the baheret and the baheret then turn it white, or since after all the hair was not white until after the baheret, it is a sign of impurity. And it appears that this matter is impossible to determine with certainty when the hair grew white, for since it comes before us white, who can say that it was not born black and the baheret turned it white in the blink of an eye? And if so, this is included as well in what we learned (Nega'im 4:1): ״a doubt whether…״ etc. And see what the Tosafot said in chapter 2 of Niddah (19a s.v. ר״ו), that the baheret's presumption is that it turns the hair white, and therefore in a case of doubt it is impure." "<b>A swelling or a scab, etc.</b> They said of blessed memory (Nega'im 1:1) that the meaning of sapachat is a kind similar to se'et — for se'et is clean wool, and that which resembles it in the second degree of gradation is like the lime of the Sanctuary. Now, even though sapachat is mentioned only alongside se'et, the same applies to the sapachat of baheret; and this is the measure of the verse: se'et or its sapachat, or baheret and its sapachat." "And there is to know why the verse preceded the mention of se'et before baheret, since baheret is a white [discoloration] than which there is none higher, as they said, of blessed memory (Negaim 1:1): baheret is intense like snow; second to it is se'et, which is like white wool. If so, it should have placed the greater rank first and then the ranks below it. And it appears that if the verse had said ״baheret or sapachat or se'et,״ on account of baheret being the most intense in its whiteness, when it would say ״or sapachat״ one would not learn from it the sapachat of se'et, and I would say that we have only three kinds of negaim, namely baheret, se'et, sapachat. Also, if it had said ״baheret or se'et or sapachat״ I would not know that there is a third kind of nega, namely sapachat of baheret, since the mention of sapachat comes last — which is not so according to the order stated." "And regarding what they said, of blessed memory (Arakhin 15b), that tzara'at comes upon lashon ha-ra, the verse alludes with two appearances of negaim according to what the Rambam wrote in chapter 3 of Hilkhot De'ot, and this is his language: lashon ha-ra is one who speaks disparagingly of his fellow even though he speaks truth; but one who speaks falsely is called a motzi shem ra upon his fellow — end quote. Now, one who speaks words of disparagement about his fellow, even though he speaks truth, is called a ba'al lashon ha-ra; and one who speaks falsely is called motzi shem ra. And corresponding to these two it said se'et or baheret: se'et corresponding to motzi shem ra, and it said se'et in the sense of (Exodus 23:1) ״לֹא תִשָּׂא שֵׁמַע שָׁוְא״; baheret corresponding to lashon ha-ra, and it used the expression baheret [bright spot], brightness, which alludes to the vindication of the things said, for they are true — yet despite this they are abhorrent and will bring about tzara'at. And since there are gradations in both aspects, therefore HaShem, blessed be He, made sapachat for se'et and for baheret, corresponding to the dimension of speech that surrounds it." "<b>And it shall be, etc., for a nega.</b> Meaning, that the principal tumah of the nega should be told by the hairs, and not the appearance of the se'et and the baheret that would declare tzara'at without the hair; therefore it said ״and it shall be, etc., for a nega,״ etc. — meaning, that which comes afterward, namely when the hair turns white."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:03.878044480Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}